  "h1": "Phase 01: Code Review Report",
  "summary": "Reviewed the identity + persistence foundation: Cloudflare Access JWT verification (`auth/jwt.rs`, `auth/mod.rs`, `auth/dev_bypass.rs`), the in-memory single-use ticket store and its WS/download consumers (`tickets/*`, `ws/mod.rs`, `download/mod.rs`), the SQLite/WAL persistence substrate (`db/mod.rs`, migration), and the walking-skeleton frontend page. The core cryptographic verification path is sound: `Validation::new(Algorithm::RS256)` is pinned explicitly with mandatory `aud`/`iss`, the JWKS decoder fails closed (`Verifier::empty()`), tokens signed with a non-registered key or carrying `alg: none` are rejected (proven by real tests, not just asserted), and the ticket store's atomic check-and-delete `consume()` is proven race-free under real thread contention.\n\nThat said, this is not a clean bill of health. The one control this phase treats as the single hardest safety invariant — \"`AUTH_MODE=dev` can never silently ship enabled\" (D-10) — is implemented as a fragile string-prefix match rather than a real address check, and the ticket store shares one map with no purpose/scope discriminator between WS-authorization tickets and download-authorization tickets, which is a live cross-channel confusion (low practical impact today only because both ticket \"kinds\" currently carry identical privilege — that will not remain true once Phase 2/3 attach resource-specific authorization to a minted ticket). Several lower-severity quality/process issues are also recorded below, including that `backend/src/tickets/mint.rs` — the actual JWT-gated ticket-minting endpoints exercised by every mint-then-consume flow in this phase — was omitted from the declared 19-file review scope despite being modified in this phase; it was read and reviewed anyway and no additional defects were found in it beyond what's already covered by the mint-related findings below.",
